IMPORT prelude

-- Probation of Offenders Act 1951 (Singapore) -- when a court may put an offender on probation or discharge them instead of
-- sentencing (ss 5, 8), the limits on a probation order and on amending it (ss 5, 6), breach and further offences (ss 7, 9),
-- compensation and who pays it (s 10), and what a probation or discharge does to the conviction (s 11).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/POA1951.txt. The deposit
-- describes itself as the "version in force from 30/12/2025"; the latest amendment annotated is Act 30 of 2019 (Children and Young
-- Persons (Amendment) Act 2019), commencement 30 December 2025, annotated against ss 5(1) proviso (a), 5(9), 6(3), 8(1) proviso (a),
-- 10(4) and 11(1) proviso. The deposit does not show what those provisions said before that amendment.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Chosen for its everyday-life relevance: probation and the absolute or conditional discharge are how many first and
-- young offenders in Singapore avoid a sentence, and s 11 decides whether the conviction follows them afterwards. SCOPED to the
-- provisions an offender meets. Not encoded: appointments and committees (ss 3, 4), approved institutions (s 12), rules (s 13), the
-- copying duties (ss 5(11), 6(4)), security for good behaviour (s 10(1)), the enforcement mechanics of s 10(3), the s 9(1) summons
-- and warrant procedure beyond who has jurisdiction, and the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 limits that s 5(7), (8) borrow (the
-- maximum community service hours are in that Code, not here, so they are not encoded).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Probation of Offenders Act 1951 -- probation and discharge`

DECLARE Court IS ONE OF
    `the General Division of the High Court`
    `a District Court`
    `a Magistrate's Court`
    `a Youth Court`

DECLARE Officer IS ONE OF
    `a Judge sitting in the General Division of the High Court`
    `a District Judge`
    `a magistrate`

DECLARE Order IS ONE OF
    `a probation order`
    `an order for conditional discharge`
    `an absolute discharge`

DECLARE Requirement IS ONE OF
    `a residence requirement`
    `unpaid community service`
    `a requirement to stay away from the scene of the offence`
    `payment of damages or compensation`

DECLARE Applicant IS ONE OF
    `the probationer`
    `the probation officer`
    `a volunteer probation officer with the Chief Probation Officer's approval`
    `a volunteer probation officer without that approval`
    `the victim`

DECLARE Payer IS ONE OF
    `the offender`
    `the offender's parent or guardian`

§§ `5(1), 8(1) -- when probation or a discharge is available`

-- s 5(1): where a person is convicted of an offence "(not being an offence the sentence for which is fixed by law)", the court may,
-- "instead of sentencing him", make a probation order. Proviso: where "a specified minimum sentence or mandatory minimum sentence of
-- imprisonment or fine or caning is prescribed by law", only if the person "(a) has attained 18 years of age but has not attained
-- 21 years of age at the time of his conviction; and (b) has not been previously convicted of any such offence ... and for this
-- purpose section 11(1) shall not apply to any such previous conviction". s 8(1) repeats the same exclusion and the same proviso for
-- an absolute or conditional discharge.
--
-- Read literally, an offender UNDER 18 convicted of a minimum-sentence offence fails proviso (a): the proviso admits only the band
-- 18 to 20. Whether a Youth Court case reaches this Act at all is a question about other Acts that this row does not answer.
GIVEN `age at conviction` IS A NUMBER, `previously convicted of a minimum-sentence offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the minimum-sentence proviso is met` IF
        `age at conviction` AT LEAST 18
    AND `age at conviction` LESS THAN 21
    AND (NOT `previously convicted of a minimum-sentence offence`)

GIVEN `sentence fixed by law` IS A BOOLEAN, `minimum sentence prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN, `age at conviction` IS A NUMBER, `previously convicted of a minimum-sentence offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `probation or a discharge is available` IF
        (NOT `sentence fixed by law`)
    AND (    (NOT `minimum sentence prescribed`)
         OR  `the minimum-sentence proviso is met` `age at conviction` `previously convicted of a minimum-sentence offence`)

§§ `5(1), 5(4), 8(1) -- periods`

-- s 5(1): supervision "for a period to be specified in the order of not less than 6 months nor more than 3 years".
-- s 5(4): where the order requires residence in an approved institution, "that period shall not extend beyond 12 months from the
-- date of the order".
-- s 8(1): a conditional discharge on condition he "commits no offence during such period, not exceeding 12 months".
GIVEN `months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the probation period is lawful` IF
    `months` AT LEAST 6 AND `months` AT MOST 36

GIVEN `months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the residence period is lawful` IF
    `months` AT MOST 12

GIVEN `months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conditional discharge period is lawful` IF
    `months` AT LEAST 0 AND `months` AT MOST 12

§§ `5(2), 5(3) -- what a probation order may require`

-- s 5(2): requirements the court "considers necessary for securing the good conduct of the offender or for preventing a repetition";
-- proviso: "the payment of sums by way of damages for injury or compensation for loss shall not be included among the requirements of
-- a probation order" (compensation is instead ordered separately under s 10(2)). s 5(3): may include (a) residence or (b) unpaid
-- community service under a community service officer. The stay-away requirement is an illustration of s 5(2), not a listed item.
GIVEN `the requirement` IS A Requirement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the requirement may be in a probation order` IF
    CONSIDER `the requirement`
    WHEN `payment of damages or compensation` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 5(5): community service only if (a) the offender is suitable "based on the mental and physical condition of the offender" and
-- (b) "suitable arrangements can be made"; s 5(6): the court "must, before including the requirement ..., call for a report from a
-- community service officer".
GIVEN `suitable in mind and body` IS A BOOLEAN, `arrangements can be made` IS A BOOLEAN, `community service officer report called for` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `community service may be required` IF
    `suitable in mind and body` AND `arrangements can be made` AND `community service officer report called for`

§§ `5(9), 5(10) -- consent and the supervising officer`

-- s 5(9): the court must explain the order "in ordinary language"; "if the offender is not less than 18 years of age the court shall
-- not make the order unless he expresses his willingness to comply". Under 18, no consent is required.
GIVEN `offender's age` IS A NUMBER, `expresses willingness` IS A BOOLEAN, `effect explained` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make the probation order` IF
        `effect explained`
    AND (`offender's age` LESS THAN 18 OR `expresses willingness`)

-- s 5(10): "Where a woman or girl is placed under the supervision of a probation officer ..., the probation officer ... shall be a
-- woman." Nothing is said about a male probationer.
GIVEN `probationer is female` IS A BOOLEAN, `supervisor is a woman` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supervisor is permitted` IF
    (NOT `probationer is female`) OR `supervisor is a woman`

§§ `6 -- discharge and amendment of a probation order`

-- s 6(1), (2): application by "the probationer" or "the probation officer or volunteer probation officer". s 6(5): "A volunteer
-- probation officer shall not make an application ... without the prior approval of the Chief Probation Officer." The victim is
-- not given a right to apply.
GIVEN `the applicant` IS A Applicant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the applicant may apply to discharge or amend` IF
    CONSIDER `the applicant`
    WHEN `a volunteer probation officer without that approval` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the victim` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 6(2) proviso (a): the court "shall not amend a probation order by reducing the probation period, or by extending that period
-- beyond the end of 3 years from the date of the original order"; (b) not so that residence in an approved institution exceeds "12
-- months in all". A shorter period is reached by discharging the order under s 6(1), not by amendment.
GIVEN `original months` IS A NUMBER, `amended months` IS A NUMBER, `total residence months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the amendment is permitted` IF
        `amended months` AT LEAST `original months`
    AND `amended months` AT MOST 36
    AND `total residence months` AT MOST 12

-- s 6(3): where the court amends "otherwise than on the application of the probationer", it shall summon him, and if he "is not less
-- than 18 years of age" shall not amend unless he expresses willingness. Proviso: not for "an order cancelling a requirement ... or
-- reducing the period of any requirement".
GIVEN `applied for by the probationer` IS A BOOLEAN, `only cancels or reduces a requirement` IS A BOOLEAN, `probationer's age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the probationer's willingness is needed for the amendment` IF
        (NOT `applied for by the probationer`)
    AND (NOT `only cancels or reduces a requirement`)
    AND `probationer's age` AT LEAST 18

-- s 6(6): where residence in an approved institution extends "beyond 6 months", the officer shall report to the court "as soon as
-- possible after the expiration of 6 months"; s 6(7): the court then reviews the residence requirement.
GIVEN `residence months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a six-month residence report is due` IF
    `residence months` GREATER THAN 6

§§ `7 -- breach of a requirement`

-- s 7(2): on proof of failure to comply, the Magistrate's Court may, "without prejudice to the continuance of the probation order",
-- impose "a fine not exceeding $1,000" or detention in prison "for a period which shall not exceed 14 days". Or, (a) if the order was
-- made by a Magistrate's Court, deal with him for the original offence as if just convicted; (b) if made by the General Division of
-- the High Court or a District Court, commit him to custody or bail until he appears before that court. s 7(2) lists no Youth Court
-- case; this row treats it as neither (a) nor (b).
GIVEN `fine` IS A NUMBER, `days detained` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the breach penalty is within the limits` IF
        `fine` AT MOST 1000
    AND `days detained` AT MOST 14
    AND (`fine` EQUALS 0 OR `days detained` EQUALS 0)

GIVEN `the court that made the order` IS A Court
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Magistrate's Court may resentence for the original offence on breach` IF
    CONSIDER `the court that made the order`
    WHEN `a Magistrate's Court` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the court that made the order` IS A Court
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the breach must be sent back to the court that made the order` IF
    CONSIDER `the court that made the order`
    WHEN `the General Division of the High Court` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a District Court` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `6(8), 7(2), 8(4) -- whether the order survives`

-- s 6(8): where a probationer "is sentenced for the offence for which he was placed on probation, the probation order shall cease to
-- have effect"; s 8(4) the same for a conditional discharge. s 7(2): a fine or 14 days' detention for breach is "without prejudice to
-- the continuance of the probation order".
GIVEN `sentenced for the original offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order continues` IF
    NOT `sentenced for the original offence`

§§ `9(2) -- who may summon for a further offence`

-- s 9(2): jurisdiction to summon or issue a warrant where a further offence was committed during the probation period or period of
-- conditional discharge lies with "(a) ... a Judge sitting in the General Division of the High Court; (b) if the order was made by a
-- District Court, a District Judge; (c) if the order was made by a Magistrate's Court or Youth Court, a magistrate".
GIVEN `the court that made the order` IS A Court
GIVETH AN Officer
DECIDE `who may summon for a further offence` IS
    CONSIDER `the court that made the order`
    WHEN `the General Division of the High Court` THEN `a Judge sitting in the General Division of the High Court`
    WHEN `a District Court` THEN `a District Judge`
    OTHERWISE `a magistrate`

§§ `10(2), 10(4) -- compensation and who pays`

-- s 10(2): on making any of the three orders the court "may ... order the offender to pay such damage for injury or compensation for
-- loss as the court thinks reasonable; but, in the case of an order made by a Magistrate's Court, the damages and compensation
-- together shall not exceed $500". No cap is stated for any other court.
GIVEN `the court making the order` IS A Court, `damages` IS A NUMBER, `compensation` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the compensation order is within the limit` IF
    CONSIDER `the court making the order`
    WHEN `a Magistrate's Court` THEN `damages` + `compensation` AT MOST 500
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 10(4): where "the offender is below 18 years of age, the order must be enforced against the offender's parent or guardian, unless
-- the court is satisfied that the parent or guardian cannot be found or that the parent or guardian has not contributed to the
-- commission of the offence by the offender by neglecting to exercise due care over the offender".
GIVEN `offender's age` IS A NUMBER, `parent cannot be found` IS A BOOLEAN, `parent did not contribute by neglect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Payer
DECIDE `who the compensation order is enforced against` IS
    IF      `offender's age` LESS THAN 18
        AND (NOT `parent cannot be found`)
        AND (NOT `parent did not contribute by neglect`)
    THEN `the offender's parent or guardian`
    ELSE `the offender`

§§ `11 -- effect on the conviction`

-- s 11(1): the conviction "shall be deemed not to be a conviction for any purpose other than the purposes of the proceedings in which
-- the order is made and of any subsequent proceedings ... under the foregoing provisions". Proviso: where the offender, "being not
-- less than 18 years of age at the time of his conviction", is placed on probation or conditionally discharged and "is subsequently
-- sentenced under this Act for that offence, this subsection shall cease to apply". An offender under 18 at conviction keeps the
-- protection even after being resentenced. An absolute discharge cannot lead to resentence.
GIVEN `the order` IS A Order, `age at conviction` IS A NUMBER, `later sentenced for that offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conviction is deemed not a conviction` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `an absolute discharge` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE (NOT `later sentenced for that offence`) OR `age at conviction` LESS THAN 18

-- s 11(2): "Without prejudice to subsection (1), the conviction ... shall IN ANY EVENT be disregarded for the purposes of any written
-- law which imposes any disqualification or disability upon convicted persons". The subsection has no proviso. Read literally (an
-- inference this row has not tested against case law), it applies even where s 11(1) has ceased to apply after resentence.
GIVEN `the order` IS A Order, `later sentenced for that offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conviction is disregarded for statutory disqualifications` IF
    TRUE

-- s 11(3): subsections (1) and (2) do not affect "(a) any right of any such offender to appeal against his conviction, or to rely
-- thereon in bar of any subsequent proceedings for the same offence".
GIVEN `the order` IS A Order
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offender may still appeal the conviction` IF
    TRUE
